.\" uudecode.1 -- maux's uudecode(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic uudecode(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as env.1. Rendered at build
.\" time to build/man/UUDECODE.TXT and staged on the play image
.\" as /usr/man/UUDECODE: `more /usr/man/UUDECODE`.
.TH UUDECODE 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
uudecode \- decode a uuencode(1) file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uudecode
[
.B \-o
.I outfile
] [
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Uudecode
reads
.I file
(or standard input) and skips any text before the first
.B begin
or
.B begin-base64
line -- tolerant of banner or mail-header noise ahead of it.
Both the historical uuencode alphabet and base64 (RFC 4648) are
accepted, distinguished by that header line; the body's trailer
(a zero-length line and
.BR end ,
or a line of
.BR ==== )
is required, matching this line of uuencode/uudecode elsewhere.
.PP
The decoded bytes are written to the pathname named in the
header, unless
.B \-o
overrides it.
.TP
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write the decoded bytes to
.I outfile
instead of the header's own pathname.
.I Outfile
of
.B /dev/stdout
writes to standard output instead of creating a file.
.PP
.I Uudecode
accepts EITHER of maux's two text conventions on input: a
high-bit maux text file (what this build's own
.I uuencode
writes) or plain 7-bit ASCII (what arrives over
.BR tip (1),
whose line reader strips the high bit before a byte ever
reaches a redirected file, docs/SERIAL.md). Every input byte is
normalized to 7 bits before use, so the same decode path reads
either convention without a flag or a guess.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/UUDECODE  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
uuencode(1), tip(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success; 1 if no
.B begin
line is ever found, if the body ends before its trailer (a
short file), if the body contains an invalid character or a
misplaced
.BR = ,
or if the output file could not be opened or created; 2 on a
usage error.
.SH BUGS
The header's mode field is read and discarded: this machine has
no permission bits to apply to the decoded file.
